WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
(applause)
- One thing you may not know

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.520
about me, is that I am
obsessed with cookbooks.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.490
I read two or three a week,

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:09.920
and I have an entire collection at home.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.500
I'm talking floor-to-ceiling shelves.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.270
By the way, that's one corner.

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:15.810
There's like many more walls of it, okay?

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.640
So I wanna create a place,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.330
where we can all share our
love of cookbooks together.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.560
So this is Cookbook Club.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.580
(audience applauds)

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:30.250
Oh, I kind of couldn't have wished

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:32.310
for a more incredible person

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.910
to kick this signature segment off with.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:39.410
He is a legend in the food world,

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:41.970
and has the biggest heart ever.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.410
His organization, World Central Kitchen,

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:49.410
has served more than
40 million fresh meals,

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:51.960
to people around the world impacted by,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.240
natural disasters and other crises.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.193
Please welcome the incredible José Andrés.

00:01:00.154 --> 00:01:03.154
(audience applauds)

00:01:06.323 --> 00:01:07.156
- Hello Drew.

00:01:07.156 --> 00:01:08.130
How are you?

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.070
- Oh my God.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:11.060
I am such an Uper fun.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:14.025
So this is our first Cookbook Club.

00:01:14.025 --> 00:01:15.410
(audience applauds)
And this came out last year.

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:18.070
I already have this in my collection.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:21.310
And can you talk about the cookbook

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.310
that you're going to
suggest for Cookbook Club,

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.840
so that you're gonna turn us all on too.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.370
- Thank you for showing that.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:31.310
But one of my favorite cookbooks,

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:32.870
I love history,

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:36.420
and I have a big collection of all books,

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.170
but for me what's very
important as an immigrant,

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:43.540
to learn more about the history of America

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.370
through its cooking.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:47.327
And this is my favorite.

00:01:47.327 --> 00:01:51.300
"The Food of a Younger Land"

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.140
of Mark Kurlansky.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.550
If you wanna know what America was eating

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:01.300
at the beginning of the 1900s,

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:04.120
here is a compilation

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.630
of stories, essays,

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:10.643
that he found in the
Library of the Congress.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:13.840
Because after the WPA,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.320
the Works Progress Administration,

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.500
I'm too young to be
alive when that happened,

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.670
they created a group called America Eats.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:25.800
Where they were paying
writers to document,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.910
what traditions were,
all across the 50 States.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:34.910
So here you're gonna be finding
amazing stories of America,

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:37.240
that America doesn't know about.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.130
- So it's an origin book,

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:40.480
a historical book,

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.400
and a book with recipes.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:44.370
I think this is an incredible way,

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.180
a profound way if you will,

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:47.600
to kick off the book club.
(audience applauds)

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:51.960
Okay, you are such an
important person on this planet

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.540
with what you're doing with your life.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.200
- Well, let me tell you Drew,

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.470
thank you, but I'm no hero.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.270
I'm just one cook,

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:04.980
that I'm very lucky to be
surrounded by amazing people.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.320
Many of them cooks, and
chefs, and volunteers that,

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:13.150
through the world, when there is a fire,

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:14.410
we show up.

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:15.770
We believe that sometimes,

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:16.990
a plate of food,

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:18.880
can be the beginning of a better tomorrow.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.460
So you will see us after hurricanes,

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:23.460
earthquakes, explosions,

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:26.490
and quite frankly I have a
team that I don't deserve.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.040
And I'm so honored to be next to them,

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.072
trying to provide food to people in need.

00:03:32.072 --> 00:03:32.905
- Well listen,

00:03:32.905 --> 00:03:36.600
really, you are a game
changer for our planet.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.450
The way you show up for people,

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:40.240
the effectiveness,

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:43.490
the galvanizing and organizing,

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:46.110
the doing that you do,

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:49.280
it is a true game changer.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.078
- Sometimes when you need
people to take care of

00:03:53.078 --> 00:03:53.960
in hurt,

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.420
after a hurricane, you
send doctors and nurses.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:58.390
When you need to rescue people,

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:01.430
you send first responders
and firefighters.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:03.920
When you need to feed
people, who you should send?

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:05.270
Well, this is simple.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:07.470
It's cooks like me, all around America,

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:08.440
all around the world,

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.530
that we put our know-how to feed,

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.870
not only the few in our restaurants,

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:16.640
but to try to feed the
many after disasters.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:17.510
Simple.

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:21.811
- Well, you are a total inspiration
to me and everyone else.

00:04:21.811 --> 00:04:24.394
(upbeat music)

